q-sunshine-pve for Proxmox VE 9
===============================

q-sunshine-pve adds a native remote-console transport for QEMU VMs. It has one
node-level `q-sunshine-terminal` service; it does not install a Sunshine Web
UI, UPnP/mDNS discovery service, PIN pairing service, per-VM systemd unit, or
a second user/password authority.

The bundled Sunshine is compiled as a transport-only worker, not a host
application server. It exposes one fixed `QEMU Console` GameStream entry and
cannot read `apps.json`, execute a host command or prep command, manage a host
desktop, retain Sunshine state, or run a tray/UI. Proxmox remains responsible
for all control-plane decisions. The profile forcibly selects QEMU Display1
DMA-BUF capture and guest-only audio while disabling every host-desktop
capture and display-control backend. Encoder selection remains a root-owned
per-VM policy: a provisioned NVIDIA host can explicitly use NVENC, while
unprovisioned hardware falls back through Sunshine's normal capability probe.
It starts no plaintext GameStream HTTP listener.

PVE remains the authorization boundary:

    PVE session + VM.Console ACL
      -> protected pvedaemon route
      -> root-only local terminal handoff
      -> one-use .qsm file -> native Qt/Moonlight transport

No PVE cookie, CSRF value, password, VNC ticket, or Sunshine pairing state is
passed to the transport. The package contains no X11, Wayland, PulseAudio,
ALSA, libcurl, or miniupnpc runtime dependency.

## 1. Install on each PVE node

Install the same package on every node that can host a q-sunshine VM:

    sudo apt install ./q-sunshine-pve_*.deb
    sudo q-sunshine-preflight --virgl --encoder
    sudo q-sunshine-pve-ui status

The package installs guarded PVE API/service and Console-menu integration. The
route is enabled only for its reviewed PVE 9 file/version profile; after a PVE
upgrade an unsupported profile falls back to stock `pveproxy`/`pvedaemon` and
the Console item is safely disabled until q-sunshine is updated.

## 2. Configure the one terminal service

Create its internal descriptor-ticket key once on the node:

    sudo install -d -o root -g root -m 0700 /etc/q-sunshine/terminal
    sudo sh -c 'umask 077; head -c 32 /dev/urandom > /etc/q-sunshine/terminal/ticket.key'

Copy and edit the root-only terminal configuration:

    sudo install -o root -g root -m 0600 \
      /usr/share/doc/q-sunshine-pve/example-terminal.conf \
      /etc/q-sunshine/terminal.conf
    sudoedit /etc/q-sunshine/terminal.conf

By default it uses `/etc/pve/local/pve-ssl.pem`,
`/etc/pve/local/pve-ssl.key`, and `/etc/pve/pve-root-ca.pem`. The shared
cluster node map is server-side policy; the browser does not receive or choose
from it:

    sudo install -o root -g root -m 0644 \
      /usr/share/doc/q-sunshine-pve/example-node-endpoints.json \
      /etc/pve/q-sunshine-node-endpoints.json
    sudoedit /etc/pve/q-sunshine-node-endpoints.json

Every node needs one reachable TLS endpoint entry. It contains public routing
metadata only; never place a PVE credential, ticket, launch claim, or private
key in it.

## 3. Prepare each QEMU VM

For VM 100 create its fixed GameStream server identity and lease authority:

    sudo q-sunshine-provision-vm 100
    sudo install -o root -g root -m 0600 \
      /usr/share/doc/q-sunshine-pve/example-vm.conf \
      /etc/q-sunshine/instances.d/100.conf
    sudoedit /etc/q-sunshine/instances.d/100.conf

The VM policy requires unique media/QSF ports, a private QEMU Display1 D-Bus
address, and all six `QSUNSHINE_GAMESTREAM_LEASE_*` values. The terminal keeps
the issuer and CA private key; Sunshine receives only the public VM CA,
audience, and pre-provisioned server certificate/key.

Before starting the VM, configure Display1 by one of these mutually exclusive
methods:

1. In the PVE QEMU creation wizard open **Display (Advanced)**, or for an
   existing VM open **Hardware → Display → Advanced**, enable **q-sunshine
   Display1**, and select the render node. The form selects `VirGL GPU` and
   manages only the exact QEMU Display1 D-Bus argument. It refuses to rewrite
   an existing custom `-display` argument. A creation-wizard VM ID must be
   fixed rather than automatically assigned.

2. For declarative/manual configuration, add the complete reviewed QEMU
   `args:` value below (adapt VMID and render node only) and set `vga: none`:

    args: -display dbus,addr=unix:path=/run/q-sunshine/100/qemu-display1.bus,gl=on,rendernode=/dev/dri/renderD128 -device virtio-vga-gl -chardev socket,id=qsf_agent,path=/run/q-sunshine/100/qsf-agent.sock,server=on,wait=off -device virtio-serial-pci,id=qsf_virtio_serial -device virtserialport,chardev=qsf_agent,name=org.qsunshine.agent -chardev socket,id=qsf_telemetry,path=/run/q-sunshine/100/qsf-telemetry.sock,server=on,wait=off -device virtserialport,chardev=qsf_telemetry,name=org.qsunshine.virgl.wayland.telemetry

The form deliberately does not create the QSF guest-agent/telemetry devices.
When using the form, put only this remaining reviewed QSF fragment in `args:`
(adapt VMID, but do not add a second `-display` or `virtio-vga-gl` device):

    args: -chardev socket,id=qsf_agent,path=/run/q-sunshine/100/qsf-agent.sock,server=on,wait=off -device virtio-serial-pci,id=qsf_virtio_serial -device virtserialport,chardev=qsf_agent,name=org.qsunshine.agent -chardev socket,id=qsf_telemetry,path=/run/q-sunshine/100/qsf-telemetry.sock,server=on,wait=off -device virtserialport,chardev=qsf_telemetry,name=org.qsunshine.virgl.wayland.telemetry

The guest must run the supplied QSF guest agent for clipboard, file transfer,
and client-driven VirGL/Wayland display profiles.


## 4. Start and use it

Enable the sole service before PVE starts a configured VM:

    sudo systemctl enable --now q-sunshine-terminal.service
    sudo systemctl status q-sunshine-terminal.service
    sudo qm start 100

In the PVE Web UI, open the QEMU VM's **Console** split menu and choose
**q-sunshine**. PVE checks the existing session and `VM.Console` permission,
then downloads one `.qsm` launch descriptor. Open it with the q-sunshine Qt
client. The descriptor may be redeemed once; after that the client obtains
short-lived, in-memory media and QSF leases. A second redemption fails.

Windowed/full-screen changes trigger a controlled media reconnect after the
guest acknowledges the requested VirGL display profile. Clipboard and files
use the separately authenticated VM QSF route, not Sunshine's host process or
PVE's VNC channel.

## Removal

Stop affected VMs before stopping the terminal, because their private Display1
bus is terminal-owned:

    sudo qm shutdown 100
    sudo systemctl disable --now q-sunshine-terminal.service
    sudo apt remove q-sunshine-pve

Removal restores the package-owned PVE UI diversion and PVE service drop-ins.
It deliberately leaves root-managed terminal configuration, VM policy, and
key material for explicit operator review.
